WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:10.760
SUBJECT: So according to Beals' obituary in the New York Times from 1942,

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:14.750
she acquired this camera as a result of it

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:19.370
being a subscription prize from a children's magazine

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:21.890
that was published in Boston.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:27.170
And this kind of camera was relatively inexpensive-- this small box camera.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:31.700
Essentially a hole in a box with, perhaps, a lens.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:34.130
I haven't examined it closely enough to know,

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:37.340
but the light's passing through the hole and projecting itself

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:40.910
onto some kind of film on the back of the camera.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:44.720
To have fallen in love with photography through this device,

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.380
means you love the elemental magic of photography.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:55.280
This is not one of our brilliant muralist digital cameras of today.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.060
This is a very basic apparatus.

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:03.560
But at this moment, a few things are happening in photography

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.470
of particular relevance to her life.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:12.510
Things that are happening that she took advantage of in an extraordinary way.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:15.740
And one has to do with the size of the camera--

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:19.010
the fact that it's shrunk down from these large boxes that

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:24.320
were used previously into this small handheld device.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.610
For most of the middle of the 19th century, cameras were large boxes.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:33.980
Quite heavy on tripods, they took a lot to lug around.

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:37.910
But beginning in the late 1880s, we have the introduction

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:40.010
of these small cameras.

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:44.150
Most famously from Kodak in 1888, although there

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:47.570
were a number of companies producing these small cameras.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:51.260
They were hand-held, they were much less conspicuous.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:55.220
They allowed the photographer to be mobile, to move around,

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:59.960
to take photographs of people, sometimes when people were not aware

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.150
that they were being photographed.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:05.540
For this reason, they were often called detective cameras

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:08.600
and a lot of ethical questions were raised about whether or not

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:12.980
photographers had become a little too inconspicuous.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:18.860
But they also afforded opportunities to capture modern life on the fly.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:21.950
So photographers, now, could walk around and snap

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:28.070
pictures of things that were just unfolding on the street in a way

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:32.390
that photographers of earlier generations couldn't do.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:34.910
A key technological development for Beals,

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:41.330
was the advent of halftone technology for reproducing photographs in print.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:44.060
For the early decades of photography, there

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:50.690
was no easy way to turn a photograph into an illustration for a newspaper

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:52.790
because it had to be converted, basically,

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:56.950
to a wood engraving, which was a technology that predated photography.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:01.010
So no labor was saved, you were just using the photograph as the model,

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:03.590
rather than a scene in real life.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:08.840
With halftone technology, a screen was used to photo mechanically

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.690
reproduce the photograph in print.

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:16.190
And this gets perfected in the 1890s, more or less.

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:19.970
So right around the time that Beals is starting to think, perhaps,

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.430
of becoming a photojournalist.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:24.620
So she's getting in on the ground floor.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:27.290
This is really a new occupation.

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:30.830
An occupation that didn't really exist prior

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:33.680
to these technological developments.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.800
So it's rather extraordinary that she managed

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:44.030
to find herself in this occupation by dint of her effort and initiative.

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.460
One thing we tend to forget today is the extent

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:50.150
to which these small cameras were associated

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:54.360
with the emergence of the bicycle, which comes out at around the same time.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:57.950
The safety bike comes out in the early 1890s.

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:00.200
Absolute revolution.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:03.290
Some people call it a fad, but they're not far seeing enough.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:05.960
The bike is a tremendous revolution.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:08.510
It's followed so quickly by the automobile.

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:11.420
That looking backward, we just focus on Henry Ford

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:13.160
and the emergence of the automobile.

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:16.435
But the emergence of the bike, when it happened was huge.

00:04:16.435 --> 00:04:21.290
And Susan B Anthony said it did more to liberate women than anything else.

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:24.480
And this notion that the world was becoming more mobile,

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.450
more accelerated-- and that this offered opportunities for liberation--

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:32.120
is something very important to remember.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:33.290
It was unsettling.

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:36.530
People were worried that the world was becoming too fast.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:38.870
That it was somehow becoming unreal.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:45.160
But I think for enterprising women, this was a time of great opportunity.

